1
00:00:05,979 --> 00:00:08,109
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:12,110
This time, let's talk about the mindset around drawing.

3
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:22,850
In Chapter 0, part 1, I asked you how you started drawing and why you started.

4
00:00:22,851 --> 00:00:26,690
Go to Chapter 0, part 1 and answer in the comments.

5
00:00:26,691 --> 00:00:35,970
As I mentioned in the welcome chapter, a big part of why I started drawing was a sense of ownership and a desire to create, but there were other reasons too.

6
00:00:35,971 --> 00:00:43,570
Back when I wasn't drawing, looking at other people's drawings, I felt both awe and a bit of envy.

7
00:00:43,571 --> 00:00:45,250
I thought I could probably do that much too.

8
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:47,090
That feeling came to me.

9
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:51,690
After a while, I fell into a slight bit of overconfidence.

10
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:55,330
I got a job not long after I started drawing.

11
00:00:55,335 --> 00:01:03,090
Looking back now, I was lucky to get hired, but back then I thought that was my real skill.

12
00:01:03,091 --> 00:01:13,131
But the overconfidence that got me started drawing, made me build a portfolio, and got me to submit my resume worked like a placebo effect.

13
00:01:13,949 --> 00:01:18,240
A moderate amount of overconfidence is indistinguishable from confidence.

14
00:01:18,241 --> 00:01:20,560
So what if my actual skill doesn't measure up right now?

15
00:01:20,561 --> 00:01:22,880
Confidence is always important.

16
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:25,280
That's true in any field.

17
00:01:25,281 --> 00:01:34,240
I often see aspiring artists around me, and the mindset of those who get a job and those who don't is quite different.

18
00:01:34,241 --> 00:01:39,120
When looking at someone else's well-drawn picture, thinking, "Can I draw like that?"

19
00:01:39,121 --> 00:01:42,080
More than thinking, "I could never get to that level,"

20
00:01:42,564 --> 00:01:48,560
You have to become the kind of person who thinks, "Someday I'll be able to draw like that too."

21
00:01:48,561 --> 00:01:51,840
Think of yourselves as great, everyone.

22
00:01:51,841 --> 00:01:55,840
Back in your school days, you were probably the ones in your class who drew the best.

23
00:01:57,109 --> 00:02:02,360
Even if not, think of yourself as the person who will become the best draw going forward.

24
00:02:02,361 --> 00:02:05,640
For you, humility is not the answer.

25
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:08,600
Hold on to a moderate amount of overconfidence.

26
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:15,800
Don't undervalue yourself just because you draw worse than the people around you right now and are falling behind.

27
00:02:15,801 --> 00:02:17,960
Life is a marathon.

28
00:02:17,961 --> 00:02:20,280
You have to live at your own pace.

29
00:02:21,589 --> 00:02:28,641
A moderate amount of overconfidence will keep you from comparing yourself to others and make you the center of your own life.

30
00:02:32,565 --> 00:02:37,310
For people who create, psychological stability is extremely important.

31
00:02:37,311 --> 00:02:40,910
Those in the arts and athletics are hit hardest by slumps.

32
00:02:40,911 --> 00:02:44,830
When a slump comes, I set everything aside and played games.

33
00:02:44,831 --> 00:02:49,470
In my early twenties, when I was preparing for jobs, I'd go months without drawing.

34
00:02:49,471 --> 00:02:56,350
Well, back then drawing wasn't everything to me, and I lived thinking, "I'm still young, so I can do it later."

35
00:02:57,790 --> 00:03:05,920
As I pursued becoming a game concept artist, I'd get this illusion that my eye for things improved while playing games, and gradually I started to want to draw again.

36
00:03:07,215 --> 00:03:14,120
When I felt that desire to draw again and I drew, somehow it seemed like it came out well and like I had improved.

37
00:03:15,364 --> 00:03:18,880
Actually, whether my skill improved or not wasn't what mattered.

38
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:23,600
What mattered was whether I regained my confidence or not.

39
00:03:23,601 --> 00:03:30,480
For me, a slump is a state where the driving force that lets me keep going at something has disappeared.

40
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,560
It's a bit like a drained battery.

41
00:03:33,875 --> 00:03:37,660
In other words, a slump comes because you've been running too hard.

42
00:03:38,839 --> 00:03:41,820
It's a slump that comes because you're desperate.

43
00:03:41,821 --> 00:03:47,620
Put another way, if you've never had a slump, take a look back and ask whether you're really working hard.

44
00:03:48,515 --> 00:03:50,430
If a slump has come, congratulations.

45
00:03:50,431 --> 00:03:52,750
You've received a slump-crushing quest.

46
00:03:53,179 --> 00:03:56,830
If you clear it, you level up.

47
00:03:56,831 --> 00:04:03,470
Since I've gone through the cycle of draining and recharging my battery several times, a slump doesn't scare me.

48
00:04:03,471 --> 00:04:04,430
Actually, I like it.

49
00:04:04,431 --> 00:04:09,790
Overcoming it makes me think I'll grow again, like something out of a boy's comic.

50
00:04:09,791 --> 00:04:18,190
If the storehouse of knowledge I've built up through practice is solid, then even if a slump comes and closes the door for a while.

51
00:04:18,570 --> 00:04:21,930
The knowledge I've stored up won't disappear.

52
00:04:21,931 --> 00:04:26,970
This is a bit extreme, but when a slump comes, just put down your pen.

53
00:04:26,971 --> 00:04:35,370
Play games, travel, do other things, and use it as a chance to broaden your horizons and build up new knowledge.

54
00:04:35,371 --> 00:04:37,450
You say you can't do that because you're a professional?

55
00:04:37,451 --> 00:04:42,810
Take a few days off, even a day, even if you have to use your annual leave, and put down your pen.

56
00:04:42,811 --> 00:04:46,730
When a slump comes and you hesitate to draw even a single line, and nothing feels right.

57
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:51,570
I'm certain that keeping your grip on the pen won't solve the slump.

58
00:04:56,625 --> 00:05:01,450
Do you feel like you're truly studying hard and practicing, but your skill isn't improving?

59
00:05:02,255 --> 00:05:06,211
People say drawing skill rises in steps like this.

60
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:13,040
It rises steeply, then there's a flat period where your skill doesn't seem to change.

61
00:05:13,041 --> 00:05:17,321
But even during this time, your skill is likely improving without you realizing it.

62
00:05:18,575 --> 00:05:21,320
Let me interpret this graph a bit.

63
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:31,441
This steeply rising part is when you pour 120% of your ability into your drawing and complete a picture through blood, sweat, and tears.

64
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:37,800
Because the skill improvement shows up as a result, you can believe your skill has improved.

65
00:05:37,801 --> 00:05:39,720
But think about it.

66
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:47,880
The time you put in to draw that 120% picture was probably much longer than when you drew a picture at 100% of your ability.

67
00:05:48,925 --> 00:05:56,520
Even so, you think of that 120% picture you achieved as your skill, and you'll put in effort toward that kind of result again.

68
00:05:57,750 --> 00:05:59,400
But it's not easy.

69
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:07,000
No matter how hard you try, it's hard to draw a better picture than the 120% one, and it even gets worse.

70
00:06:08,735 --> 00:06:10,010
Actually, it didn't get worse.

71
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:12,730
It's just 110% of your ability.

72
00:06:12,731 --> 00:06:17,610
But since the difference in results is visible, you're bound to feel psychologically deflated.

73
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:21,370
This is what we think of as a slump.

74
00:06:21,371 --> 00:06:36,490
But if you keep trying without giving up even in this section, the 120% picture will become a 100% picture of your ability, and the work time it takes to complete that level of picture will also shrink.

75
00:06:36,491 --> 00:06:37,491
Once that happens.

76
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:41,171
You'll be ready to rise steeply again.

77
00:06:42,135 --> 00:06:52,361
In other words, this flat section is when you get used to drawing at a level above your ability and your work speed increases.

78
00:06:53,765 --> 00:07:02,720
Once you reach a certain level, your work speed just gets faster, and it becomes hard to find a chance to climb back onto that steep slope.

79
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:07,660
Enjoy climbing the stairs.

80
00:07:07,661 --> 00:07:09,500
You'll miss it later.

81
00:07:14,455 --> 00:07:17,920
There are people who link drawing with talent.

82
00:07:17,921 --> 00:07:25,920
In the arts, talent, especially in drawing, is considered to include both a sense-based ability and a technical ability.

83
00:07:25,921 --> 00:07:30,240
Let me first talk about the sense-based talent in drawing.

84
00:07:30,241 --> 00:07:54,360
If you had true innate sense, you'd discover and create those elements we'd call "sense," like design, composition, pose, color, and so on, on your own. But at this point, decades after game concept art came into being, I think what matters more is how well you can combine all the accumulated data.

85
00:07:54,361 --> 00:08:00,040
Concept art is already oversaturated, and similar concepts are overflowing.

86
00:08:00,041 --> 00:08:14,241
In the end, rather than an artist with the top 0.01% original sense, a concept artist who can flexibly adapt to the trends of the moment is competitive in most cases.

87
00:08:14,805 --> 00:08:21,481
It'd be great if what I created became the trend itself, but reality isn't that easy.

88
00:08:21,735 --> 00:08:25,561
Ideals and reality always tend to be far apart.

89
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:30,480
Let me also talk about the technical side.

90
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:34,960
Some people get obsessed with the presence or absence of talent.

91
00:08:34,961 --> 00:08:37,679
What on earth is talent?

92
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:43,560
What they're calling talent, I think, often refers to the technical side.

93
00:08:44,005 --> 00:08:51,840
It's the blunt kind of talk like, "Someone studied for three years and got a job, but someone else studied for five years and still can't get hired."

94
00:08:51,841 --> 00:09:03,040
The quickest path to getting hired as a concept artist is, as I mentioned before, building the ability to flexibly draw in line with the trends.

95
00:09:03,041 --> 00:09:07,120
That can be considered largely part of the technical domain.

96
00:09:07,555 --> 00:09:13,200
The time it takes to acquire this technical side varies wildly from person to person.

97
00:09:13,201 --> 00:09:18,160
I think the ability to quickly grasp something is also a talent.

98
00:09:18,161 --> 00:09:22,080
This kind of talent is closely related to the technical side.

99
00:09:22,081 --> 00:09:24,720
In the end, the technical side isn't talent.

100
00:09:25,245 --> 00:09:27,640
What's talent is how quickly you acquire it.

101
00:09:28,945 --> 00:09:32,970
People don't call an ability that anyone can obtain a talent.

102
00:09:32,971 --> 00:09:38,410
The technical skill and sense needed to get a job can be obtained by anyone, given enough time.

103
00:09:38,411 --> 00:09:39,411
Really.

104
00:09:40,255 --> 00:09:49,500
These days there are tons of YouTube lessons, tons of academies, and overflowing with capable instructors, so it's a truly great time to learn drawing.

105
00:09:49,501 --> 00:09:56,620
For the technical side of drawing, there are countless instructors, books, and videos that lead you along.

106
00:09:56,621 --> 00:10:02,620
Even if you think you're not technically gifted, try to ease some of that worry.

107
00:10:02,621 --> 00:10:05,820
I mean, you can still be full of sensitivity.

108
00:10:05,821 --> 00:10:09,621
The technical part may be slower, but you can definitely learn it.

109
00:10:10,735 --> 00:10:14,721
The path to a job through drawing is like a hiking trail.

110
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,440
But it's not as tall a mountain as you might think.

111
00:10:18,441 --> 00:10:27,400
There are people with the talent to climb fast, while others will climb markedly slower than everyone else.

112
00:10:27,401 --> 00:10:29,960
But in the end, you all meet at the summit.

113
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:35,000
Even if you climb fast, there's nowhere higher to go.

114
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:38,481
You get why concept art is a red ocean, right?

115
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:45,790
But because of various circumstances, many people get exhausted halfway, give up, and leave drawing as a hobby.

116
00:10:45,791 --> 00:10:46,910
Especially because of making a living.

117
00:10:47,710 --> 00:10:51,070
It's unfortunate, but it's also an unavoidable part of it.

118
00:10:51,071 --> 00:10:55,470
In times like this, you can only envy those talented people with fast learning ability.

119
00:10:57,039 --> 00:11:00,880
It's a cliche, but it's a matter of talent, like English proficiency.

120
00:11:00,881 --> 00:11:03,760
Especially in concept art.

121
00:11:03,761 --> 00:11:06,480
It's not as hard a path as you might think.

122
00:11:06,481 --> 00:11:07,520
As long as you have passion.

123
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:12,060
There's a saying that your hobby and your job can become one.

124
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:22,140
It means your passion and your profession align, and I think drawing is the job that best fits that saying.

125
00:11:22,141 --> 00:11:26,140
How happy is it that you can earn money while drawing what you love?

126
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:31,740
People say drawing is most enjoyable when it's a hobby, but that's nonsense.

127
00:11:32,380 --> 00:11:36,220
Unless you're a building owner, you have to work anyway.

128
00:11:36,221 --> 00:11:39,100
Isn't it better to do it with something you love?

129
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:41,660
You can do it for work and as a hobby at the same time.

130
00:11:42,970 --> 00:11:47,260
This isn't a grand speech, but for the sake of that unity of passion and work, burn your passion.

131
00:11:48,470 --> 00:11:52,520
It will surely spur you on more effectively than some people who only have talent.

132
00:11:54,085 --> 00:11:57,840
If you have both talent and passion, you wouldn't even hesitate, so I'll skip you.

133
00:12:00,014 --> 00:12:05,921
I think many of the people taking this class are truly passionate.

134
00:12:06,490 --> 00:12:10,300
There probably aren't many who think that just spending money will automatically improve their skill.

135
00:12:12,405 --> 00:12:15,230
Not many people draw well from the start.

136
00:12:15,231 --> 00:12:21,550
The worries you're having, I and other professional concept artists have them too.

137
00:12:21,630 --> 00:12:29,150
The difference between those who move forward and those who don't is the difference between making an effort to solve your worries and not making that effort.

138
00:12:30,305 --> 00:12:35,340
Let me wrap up by revealing a picture I drew back when I was preparing for jobs.

139
00:12:35,341 --> 00:12:38,620
I really didn't want to show this, but you can look at it in secret.

140
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:44,520
I hope you gain confidence from seeing this.

141
00:12:44,521 --> 00:12:51,160
This time, we talked about the kinds of worries you might have while drawing and the mindset for it.

142
00:12:51,161 --> 00:12:56,040
Next time, let's look at stories about surviving as a professional in the industry.
